response:
The GELA LNH-93-1 study demonstrated that for patients ≤60 years with low-risk stages I–II intermediate-risk non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL), **aggressive chemotherapy (ACVBP followed by consolidation) resulted in a higher response rate and a lower recurrence rate compared to standard chemotherapy (CHOP) plus radiotherapy (RT).**  

The key findings include:  
- **Higher overall survival (OS)** was observed in the ACVBP group, with a median OS of 10.5 years versus 9.5 years in the CHOP/RT group.  
- The ACVBP group had a **higher median progression-free duration (PFS)** of 10.5 years compared to 8.5 years in the CHOP/RT group.  
- The ACVBP group had a **lower recurrence rate** (10% vs. 15%).  

These findings suggest that aggressive chemotherapy (ACVBP followed by consolidation) is a more effective treatment option for patients ≤60 years with low-risk stages I–II intermediate-risk NHL compared to standard chemotherapy (CHOP) plus radiotherapy (RT).  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Aggressive chemotherapy (ACVBP)} \\
&\quad \text{vs. Standard chemotherapy (CHOP) + Radiotherapy (RT)} \\
&\text{For patients ≤60 years with low-risk stages I–II intermediate-risk NHL.}
\end{aligned}
}

