The terms "isotopologues' and "isotopomers" are used to describe molecules with different isotopic compositions. The key difference lies in the number of atoms involved in the substitution:  
1. **Isotopologues**: This term refers to molecules that have the same molecular formula but differ in the isotopic composition of one or more atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O} \) can have isotopologues like \( \text{C}_6^{12}\text{H}_2^{16}\text{O} \), \( \text{C}_6^{12}\text{H}_2^{17}\text{O} \), or \( \text{C}_6^{13}\text{H}_2^{16}\text{O} \).  
2. **Isotopomers**: This term is used when the isotopic substitution affects the structure of the molecule. For example, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O} \) can have isotopomers like \( \text{C}_6^{12}\text{H}_2^{16}\text{O} \) and \( \text{C}_6^{13}\text{C}^{12}\text{H}_2^{16}\text{O} \). Here, the substitution of \( ^{13}\text{C} \) changes the structure of the molecule.  

Thus, isotopologues are broader than isotopomers. All isotopomers are isotopologues, but not all isotopologues are isotopmers.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Isotopologues: Same molecular formula, different isotopic composition.} \\
&\text{Isotopomers: Same molecular formula, different isotopic composition and structure.}
\end{aligned}
}

